    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented shared-household support for `POST /api/owners`. The `sharesHousehold` flag already existed (it skipped the 409 conflict check); I added the missing `householdId` assignment and return.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `householdId` string to the `Owner` schema (so it appears on `OwnerDto`/responses but not on the writable `OwnerFields` request body).\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `householdId` field (`@Column household_id`) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **DB schemas** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added the `household_id` column to the `owners` table. Tests use hsqldb.\n\n4. **New step `owner/AssignHousehold.java`** \u2014 When the request opts in with `sharesHousehold: true` and an existing owner shares the household (same lastName + address, normalized case-insensitively with collapsed whitespace, matching `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique`), it assigns a **stable** `householdId` derived deterministically as `HH-` + first 12 hex chars of `SHA-256(normalizedLastName|normalizedAddress)`. Both the joining owner and every existing household member are updated with the same value, so order doesn't matter.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 Wired `assignHousehold` into the pipeline between `build` and `assignCustomerCode`, under `govern: [ transaction ]` so the updates to existing owners commit atomically with the new owner.\n\nBecause the identifier is derived from normalized fields, the two joiners in the test (`b` and `c`, both matching `a`) receive an identical, non-blank `householdId`, which is returned via `GET /api/owners/{id}` (`RespondWithOwner` \u2192 `OwnerMapper` maps the new field automatically).\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` test and the full suite pass.",
